// Fixture for run_target_test. Deliberately contains the three things a target
// can be got wrong on:
//
//   - a `Usize`, which is pointer-width, and which std.io prints through a
//     `value as U64` that is a no-op on a 64-bit target and a real widening on
//     wasm32. If sema and codegen disagree about how wide Usize is, that cast
//     compiles to nothing and the module fails verification.
//   - a struct with a pointer-width `Usize` between two scalars, so a member
//     offset moves when the pointer width does. It used to be the `String` that
//     carried this, back when a String *was* one pointer. The fat `{ptr, i64}`
//     is 16 bytes and 8-aligned on both targets, so host and wasm32 agreed on
//     its offset and the assertion quietly stopped being able to fail. A
//     `Usize` is 8 bytes here and 4 on wasm32, so every member after it moves --
//     and it moves for the same reason the rest of this fixture exists, since
//     `Usize` is exactly what sema and codegen have to agree about.
//   - I/O, so std.io's bodies are in the module and there is something for the
//     linker to resolve on a real cross build.

import std.io

struct Mixed {
    tag: Int,
    span: Usize,
    name: String,
    count: I64
}

// Two fields, and that is the point. The layout search may reorder everything
// after field 0, and on `Mixed` it does exactly what hides this: it sorts the
// members by size, so the one member whose size follows the pointer width ends
// up last on wasm32 and every offset before it matches the host's. With one
// field left to place there is no order to choose, so `width` sits at bit 64
// here and bit 32 on wasm32 and no reordering can make those agree.
struct Span {
    tag: Int,
    width: Usize
}

fn main() -> Int {
    let m = Mixed { tag: 1, span: 4, name: "cross", count: 7 }
    let s = Span { tag: 2, width: 6 }
    let width: Usize = 8
    println(m.name)
    println(width)
    println(s.width)
    return m.tag - 1
}
